# {{NPM_NAME}}

JavaScript and TypeScript client for Crawlora's hosted {{DISPLAY_NAME}} API.
It calls [Crawlora](https://crawlora.net?utm_source=npm&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=platform-clients&utm_content={{PLATFORM}}-javascript-homepage); it does not run a browser or scrape {{DISPLAY_NAME}} locally. A Crawlora account and `CRAWLORA_API_KEY` are required, and API use is billed under your Crawlora account. Crawlora is independent from and not endorsed by {{DISPLAY_NAME}} or its owners.

## Install

```sh
npm install {{NPM_NAME}}
```

## Get an API key

Create an account at [crawlora.net](https://crawlora.net/signup?utm_source=npm&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=platform-clients&utm_content={{PLATFORM}}-javascript-signup), then open the [Crawlora console](https://crawlora.net/app?utm_source=npm&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=platform-clients&utm_content={{PLATFORM}}-javascript-console) for API-key setup. Set your key in the shell before running the client:

```sh
export CRAWLORA_API_KEY="your-crawlora-api-key"
```

## Use

Save this example as `example.mjs`, then run `node example.mjs` after installing the package and setting your API key.

```js
import process from "node:process";
import { {{CLASS_NAME}} } from "{{NPM_NAME}}";

const apiKey = process.env.CRAWLORA_API_KEY;
if (!apiKey) throw new Error("Set CRAWLORA_API_KEY before running this example.");
const client = new {{CLASS_NAME}}({ apiKey });
{{JS_README_CALLS}}
```

The client also exports `Client` as an alias for `{{CLASS_NAME}}`. Operation
methods are available directly in camelCase and through the `{{PLATFORM}}`
group. See the full method and parameter list in the [online reference]({{DOCS_URL}}).

Methods return promises and can be awaited. See the [runnable example]({{JS_EXAMPLE_URL}}) for additional endpoint examples and text transcript output where supported.

## Configuration

Pass your key through `apiKey` or set `CRAWLORA_API_KEY` and read it from the
environment. Keep credentials out of source control and logs. Requests are
made to Crawlora's hosted API; response data and availability follow that
service's current contract.
